come now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week no matter  where people work there's always the  possibility of a health emergency  suddenly occurring but how prepared are  we to handle it  two new surveys commissioned by the  American Heart Association looked at the  issue one asked employees from multiple  fields about their level of first aid  and CPR training more than 85 percent  thought that this type of training is  important to keep the work environment  safe  however 33 percent said they never  received such training from a work  environment or any other source CBS News  contributor dr. taryn Rula is here to  tell us more about the survey Tara good  morning morning Dana so multiple  industries when they were looking at  this training what did the results show  so this is so important because one of  the worst things you can feel is that  helpless sense that somebody is down and  I don't know what to do and in fact  there are about 10,000 cardiovascular  arrests that occur every year in the  workplace so this is really a big gap  that can be filled by employers to  improve workplace safety CPR can attend  double or triple survival rates when  done effectively so when they looked  across industries what they found is  that there were big variations so  education 63% of the workers in  education said that they got training  from their current employer that is  opposed to about 19% for instance in the  hospitality or service industry so again  big variations and also 55% of those who  said they got trained it was not from  their current employer so a lot of room  for improvement if employers offer  training the the survey show that  employees actually want that training  they do want it overwhelmingly the  majority over 90 percent in this survey  said they would want the training they  felt it would improve their ability to  deal with an emergency and not many of  them expressed hesitation about getting  training about 46 percent said they  would have no hesitation and if there  were those that did express hesitation  it was because they would have to either  come to work early or leave late or  maybe they felt uncomfortable practicing  in front of colleagues or had lack of  management's yeah along those lines was  there a certain way that they wanted to  get it online or in person anything like  that you would think maybe online  because that's easier but in fact the  majority wanted to get it in person  hands-on in a classroom typesetting what  are the other things the survey found is  that employees said that if their if  their jobs offered this that they would  feel like they trusted their employer  more they would feel more positive about  their employer and more likely to stay  with them longer so you know this is  really a great opportunity for employers  because what you don't want and what  usually happens is something happens  that's bad and somebody dies or suffers  a cardiac arrest and then suddenly the  change happens in the workplace be  proactive instead of reactive we move on  to our next topic which is childhood  obesity according to the Centers for  Disease Control from 2011 to 2014 around  17% of children and adolescents aged 2  to 19 were considered obese that equates  to about 12 point 7 million children  nationwide this week the US Preventive  Services Task Force released its updated  recommendations on screening children  for obesity Tara what did the force  recommend so the recommendations are  similar to those in 2010 and that is  that children 6 or older should be  screened for obesity and if they're  found to be obese then their doctor  should refer them for comprehensive  intensive behavioral interventions and  that's been shown to help improve the  maintenance of weight loss if they get  over 26 hours over the course of 2 to 12  months and it's so important because we  know that about 80% of those children  who are obese will go on to become obese  adults they're at risk for depression  sleep apnea asthma cardiovascular risk  factors teasing bullying very important  I live this so I know this very well  when you talk about those interventions  what does that mean what does that  encompass so they talked about targeting  the child alone or the parents alone or  both together talked about supervised  physical activity sessions educating  them on nutrition and diet reading food  labels which I do with my five-year-old  daughter she knows how to read the label  and really teaching them about limits  limiting screen time limiting access to  tempting foods and goal-setting so you  know kids are really sponges they want  to learn they can learn and there's no  reason we shouldn't be targeting them  this is one of the best tools and skills  we can give our kids it will help them  for the rest of their life  no doubt about it finally we're going to  walk into the future years from now if  you ever need help getting around it  could be from the form of an exoskeleton  research published in the journal  science shows the development of a  personalized exoskeleton system it can  adapt to the way you walk even helping  to minimize the energy your body spends  it was a small trial but results showed  wearing this exoskeleton on just one  ankle reduced average energy expenditure  by about 24% they open the future this  technology can help improve the mobility  for a range of people from those  suffering from strokes to injured  athletes this is really remarkable yeah  so it's not just about creating a bionic  human but it's really about helping  those who've had injuries or accidents  and with the nice thing about this one  is that they tailored it to the person  so some of these exoskeletons if they're  not individualized they actually can  cause you to to increase your energy  expenditure but this one because it  works with your body actually decreases  the amount of energy you're expending  great information dr. taryn Rula thank  you
 

